  index.add(
      {
        id: 12,
        href: "/docs/tasks/bail-prediction-bail/",
        title: "Bail Prediction (BAIL)",
        description: "BAIL requires to predict whether the accused should be granted bail or not, given a case document (including the facts)",
        content: " Type of Task Binary Text Classification Dataset HLDC (Kapoor et al., 2022) Language Hindi No. of documents 900k Evaluation Metric macro-F1 Dataset Model Code Task Motivation and Description #\rMajority of the pending cases in the Indian legal system are from the district-level courts. Moreover, among the pending cases in district courts, a significant chunk of cases have to do with bail application (Kapoor et al., 2022). Many of the district courts in India use regional language as the official language. Since Hindi is the most spoken language in India and the majority of the courts in northern India use Hindi, in this task, we focus on the task of Bail Prediction for Hindi legal documents.\nFormally, given a legal document (having the facts of the case), the task of Bail Prediction involves predicting if the accused should be granted bail or not (i.e., a binary decision of 0 and 1).\nDataset #\rFor this task, we use Hindi Legal Document Corpus (HLDC) created by us in our previous work Kapoor et al., (2022). HLDC is a corpus of 900k Hindi legal documents from district courts of a north Indian state. HLDC corpus creation process involves various pre-processing steps to take care of possible ethical consequences that may creep in due to different types of biases. Bail documents are annotated with ground truth bail decisions.\nDataset Format #\rThe dataset directory structure is as follows:\nHLDC +-- complete_data.zip // all case docs in raw format after OCR and NER removal +-- non_bail_data.zip // all case docs not pertaining to bail applications +-- bail_data.zip // all case docs pertaining to bail decisions +-- model_data.zip // all case docs segmented into facts, arguments and ratio; used for model +-- DATA_README.md +-- sample_read_zipped_data.py // script to read a sample zip file Only model_data.zip contains data that can be processed by a bail prediction method. On extraction, we get the following structure:\nmodel_data +-- all_districts | +-- train_split_alldistricts.json | +-- val_split_alldistricts.json | +-- test_split_alldistricts.json +-- specific_districts +-- train_split_44_districts.json +-- val_split_10_districts.json +-- test_split_17_districts.json Each json has the following format:\nDict{ String: Dict{ // Each String is a numerical index for case id 'decision': String // String is either \u0026quot;dismissed/granted\u0026quot; (0/1 label) 'segments': Dict{ // document divided into rhetorical segments 'jugde-opinion': List[String] // each String is a sentence in judge opinion part 'facts-and-arguments': List[String] // each String is a sentence in facts and arguments part } } 'district': String // name of district court 'case_number': String // bail application no. } Task Evaluation #\rSince the Bail prediction task is essentially a binary prediction task, it is evaluated using the standard macro-F1 score metric.\nBaseline Models #\rThe baseline model for the task of Bail Prediction is a Multi-Task Learning-based model. The main task is the binary bail prediction, and the auxiliary task is predicting the salient sentences in the bail document. The base model used is IndicBERT since the documents are in Hindi. The model for Bail Prediction has an F1 score of 81%.\nTwo separate data splits are considered:\n(i) all-districts: train, dev and test contain files from all districts\n(ii) district-wise: dev and test contain files from districts not present in train\nFor more details, please refer to our paper Kapoor et al. (2022).\nResults #\rModel District-wise m-F1 All Districts m-F1 Doc2Vec + SVM 0.69 0.77 Doc2Vec + XGBoost 0.59 0.57 IndicBERT - first 512 0.62 0.71 IndicBERT - last 512 0.60 0.76 TF-IDF + IndicBERT 0.74 0.81 TextRank + IndicBERT 0.74 0.81 Salience Pred. + IndicBERT 0.74 0.78 Multi-Task 0.77 0.78 "
      }
    );
